# v5.2.2 (audit A9): how to cite the simulation. The version field is set by dev/tools/set_version.py and checked by dev/tools/check_versions.js.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this simulation, please cite it as below, with the version you used. The math and code of this simulation were engineered by
  Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, from the concepts in Duke Johnson's book Better To Best and his related vision for eradicating
  extreme poverty while enriching cultures and supporting human flourishing. The model has not yet been reviewed by an independent
  economist; the full code is open for anyone to check, and expert collaborators are welcome.
type: software
title: Compassionism Framework Simulation
abstract: >-
  An open, in-browser dynamic microsimulation, with a reduced-form price level, of the Compassionism framework (Creative Currency
  Octaves, Public Trust Foundations, Public Trust Housing, Social Zone Harmonization and the Citizens Internet Portal). Release v5.9
  (engine 104691f, frozen and registered on OSF before it ran, Nov 14, 2026) follows 500 simulated US adults and their children, in
  households with SCF 2022 balance sheets and a weighted top tail of wealth, for 20 or 40 years, with the programme and with no
  programme on the same random draws, in three environments, and reports poverty, basic living, wealth, work, prices and cost. Its
  main row assumes that every dollar the programme pays is matched by new output (the design's statement, a design parameter); below
  that the model has no limit on what the economy can supply. The main results average 500 paired runs; the central-bank and history
  studies use 100. harness.js reproduces them in Node; REPRODUCE.md gives every command and fingerprint.
